CH = dict(num=3, title="The World of Metals and Non-metals", theme="Properties, rusting, oxides, uses of elements",
intro="Yashwant and Anandi visit a village ironsmith, Sudarshan uncle, who heats iron in a coal furnace and hammers it into tawas, buckets, axes and tools. This raises questions about why metals can be beaten into shape, how they differ from other materials, and how they behave in air and water. The chapter classifies elements into metals and non-metals using their physical and chemical properties.",
big_questions=["Why can iron be hammered into an axe while coal crumbles?", "Why are cooking vessels metal but their handles wood or plastic?", "What exactly is rust and how can we stop it?", "Why are metal oxides basic and non-metal oxides acidic?"],
blocks=[
("h2", "4.1 Properties of Materials"),
("p", "Items tested in the chapter's activities are copper, aluminium, an iron nail, coal, sulfur (gandhak) and wood. Their behaviour under hammering, sound, heat and electricity reveals the physical properties that separate **metals** from **non-metals**."),
("h3", "4.1.1 Malleability and lustre (Activity 4.1)"),
("table", ["Material", "Lustrous?", "Hard?", "On hammering"], [["Copper", "Yes", "Hard", "Flattens"], ["Aluminium", "Yes", "Hard", "Flattens"], ["Iron nail", "Yes", "Hard", "Flattens"], ["Coal", "No", "Less hard", "Breaks into pieces (brittle)"], ["Sulfur", "No", "Soft", "Breaks into pieces (brittle)"], ["Wood", "No", "Less hard", "Neither flattens nor breaks"]]),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Metallic lustre** is the shine of a metal's surface. **Malleability** is the property by which a material can be beaten into thin sheets. Materials that break into pieces on hammering are **brittle**."),
("ul", ["Examples of malleability: silver foil on sweets, aluminium foil for wrapping food.", "**Gold and silver** are the most malleable metals.", "Not every metal is hard or solid: **sodium and potassium** are so soft that they can be cut with a knife, and **mercury** is liquid at room temperature (used in thermometers).", "**Holistic lens:** Harappans used copper and gold but hardly any iron. Iron came into everyday use later; its strength made ploughs and tools much better, which advanced Indian civilisation."]),
("h3", "4.1.2 Ductility"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Ductility** is the property by which a material can be drawn into wires. It is mainly a property of metals."),
("ul", ["Uses: electrical wires (copper, aluminium), ornaments, strings of the veena, sitar, violin and guitar, tea strainers.", "**Gold is so ductile that 1 g can be drawn into a wire 2 km long.**", "Coal and sulfur are not ductile.", "Steel (a mixture of iron, a metal, and carbon, a non-metal) wire ropes carry heavy loads in suspension bridges and cranes."]),
("h3", "4.1.3 Sonority (Activity 4.2)"),
("p", "Drop a metal spoon, coin, piece of coal and wooden block from a height. Metals make a **ringing sound**; coal and wood make a dull sound. The ability of metals to produce a ringing sound is **sonority**; metals are **sonorous**. School bells and ghungroos ring because of this."),
("h3", "4.1.4 Conduction of heat (Activity 4.3)"),
("steps", [("Set up", "Fill a glass tumbler with hot water (be careful)."), ("Immerse", "Put a metal spoon and a wooden spoon of similar size at the same time and leave for a few minutes."), ("Touch", "Carefully touch the upper ends. The metal spoon is hotter.")]),
("p", "Conclusion: heat passes easily along the metal spoon, but poorly along the wooden spoon. The transfer of heat from one point to another through a material is **conduction**. **Metals are good conductors of heat**; wood is a poor conductor. So cooking vessels are metal, but handles are wood or other poor conductors."),
("h3", "4.1.5 Conduction of electricity (Activity 4.4)"),
("p", "Make the tester circuit from the previous chapter and test aluminium foil, iron nail, sulfur, copper wire, coal, dry wood, stone, rubber eraser and nylon rope. The bulb glows only with **aluminium, iron and copper**, so all materials that conduct electricity are metals."),
("ul", ["**Good conductors of electricity** let current pass easily; **poor conductors** do not.", "Screwdriver handles are plastic and electricians wear rubber gloves and shoes because plastic and rubber are poor conductors and protect from shocks."]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Metals are generally hard, lustrous, malleable, ductile, sonorous and good conductors of heat and electricity.** Exceptions: sodium and potassium are soft; mercury is a liquid."),
("h2", "4.2 Effect of Air and Water on Metals: Iron"),
("p", "Iron objects left in the open develop a brown deposit. Activity 4.5 finds out which conditions are needed."),
("table", ["Bottle", "Condition", "Air present?", "Water present?", "Rust?"], [["A", "Nail with silica gel (dry air), tightly capped", "Yes (dry)", "No", "No"], ["B", "Nail in boiled, cooled water with oil layer on top, tightly capped", "No", "Yes", "No"], ["C", "Nail partly dipped in water, open", "Yes", "Yes", "Yes"]]),
("ul", ["Silica gel keeps air dry; boiled water has no dissolved gases; oil prevents air from dissolving in water.", "Observe for 8–10 days. Rust forms only in C."]),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Rust** is the brown deposit on iron formed in the presence of **both air (oxygen) and water (moist air)**; the process is **rusting**. **Corrosion** is the gradual damage of metal surfaces by air, water or other substances."),
("ul", ["Other examples of corrosion: green coating on copper, black coating on silver.", "Rusting costs a huge amount in replacing or repairing iron structures.", "**Prevention:** painting, oiling, greasing, and **galvanisation** (coating iron with zinc).", "**Iron Pillar of Delhi:** made in the time of Chandragupta II over 1600 years ago, about 8 m high, weighs over 6000 kg, and has barely rusted, showing India's advanced metal technology."]),
("h2", "4.3 Effect of Air and Water on Other Metals"),
("steps", [("Burn magnesium (teacher demonstration)", "Clean a 3–4 cm magnesium ribbon with sandpaper, hold it with tongs and ignite it. Wear protective goggles and keep distance."), ("Observe", "It burns with a **dazzling white flame** to leave a white powder, **magnesium oxide**, formed from magnesium and oxygen of air."), ("Test the product", "Dissolve the powder in a few drops of warm water and test with litmus. It turns **red litmus blue**, so it is **basic**.")]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Oxides of metals are generally basic in nature.**"),
("ul", ["**Sodium** reacts vigorously with oxygen and water giving a lot of heat, so it is stored in **kerosene** to keep out air and moisture."]),
("h2", "4.4 Substances that Behave Differently from Metals"),
("steps", [("Burn sulfur (teacher demonstration, ventilated area)", "Burn powdered sulfur in a deflagrating spoon and lower it into a covered gas jar. Burning sulfur produces harmful gas."), ("Dissolve the gas", "After 3–4 minutes remove the spoon, add a little water and shake."), ("Test", "The solution turns **blue litmus red**, so it is **acidic**. Sulfur + oxygen &rarr; sulfur dioxide; sulfur dioxide + water &rarr; sulfurous acid."), ("Sulfur with water", "Sulfur powder in water shows no reaction.")]),
("ul", ["Sulfur and phosphorus are **non-metals**. **Phosphorus** catches fire in air, so it is stored **under water**.", "Non-metals are usually soft and dull, neither malleable nor ductile, not sonorous, and poor conductors of heat and electricity. Examples: oxygen, hydrogen, nitrogen, carbon, sulfur, phosphorus.", "**Oxides of non-metals are acidic.** Non-metals generally do not react with water.", "Plastic, glass, wood, rubber and paper are **not** metals or non-metals because they are not elements.", "An **element** is a substance that cannot be broken down into simpler substances; **118 elements** are known, some artificial. Metals and non-metals are sub-categories of elements."]),
("table", ["Property", "Metals", "Non-metals"], [["Lustre", "Lustrous", "Generally dull"], ["Hardness", "Generally hard (exceptions: Na, K)", "Generally soft (carbon as diamond is hard)"], ["Malleable / ductile", "Yes", "No; brittle if solid"], ["Sonority", "Sonorous", "Not sonorous"], ["Heat and electricity", "Good conductors", "Poor conductors"], ["Oxide", "Basic (turns red litmus blue)", "Acidic (turns blue litmus red)"], ["With water", "Some react (Na vigorously)", "Generally do not react"], ["Examples", "Iron, copper, aluminium, gold, sodium", "Sulfur, carbon, oxygen, nitrogen, phosphorus"]]),
("h2", "4.5 Are Non-metals Essential in Everyday Life?"),
("table", ["Non-metal", "Importance"], [["Oxygen", "Needed for breathing and survival"], ["Carbon", "Building block of life; in proteins, fats, carbohydrates"], ["Nitrogen", "Making fertilisers; nutrient for plant growth"], ["Chlorine", "Water purification"], ["Iodine", "Solution used as antiseptic on wounds"]]),
("p", "**Science and Society:** alloys (mixtures of two or more metals, or a metal and a non-metal) are used in utensils, tools and industry. Special metals are used in atomic energy (zirconium) and aerospace (titanium). In India iron and aluminium are widely **recycled** to cut waste."),
("h2", "Likely exam questions"),
("qa", [("What are malleability and ductility?", "Malleability is the ability to be beaten into thin sheets; ductility is the ability to be drawn into wires."),
("Name the two most malleable metals.", "Gold and silver."),
("Name a metal that is liquid at room temperature and metals that can be cut with a knife.", "Mercury is liquid; sodium and potassium are soft."),
("Why are cooking vessels metal but handles wood/plastic?", "Metals conduct heat well, so food heats up; wood/plastic are poor conductors, so handles stay cool."),
("What is the lesson of the rusting experiment?", "Rust forms only when iron is in contact with both air and water."),
("How can rusting be prevented?", "Painting, oiling, greasing, galvanisation (zinc coating)."),
("Why is sodium kept in kerosene and phosphorus in water?", "Sodium reacts vigorously with air/moisture; phosphorus catches fire in air."),
("What happens when magnesium burns and what is the product's nature?", "Burns with white flame to form magnesium oxide, which is basic (turns red litmus blue)."),
("What happens when sulfur burns and the product is dissolved in water?", "Sulfur dioxide forms and gives sulfurous acid, which is acidic (turns blue litmus red)."),
("Why is sulfur unsuitable for galvanisation?", "A protective coating needs a metal like zinc; sulfur is a brittle non-metal, not a coating metal."),
("Why does an ironsmith heat iron before shaping?", "Hot iron becomes soft and malleable, so it can be hammered easily into tools."),
("Which non-metals are essential in daily life and why?", "Oxygen (respiration), carbon (life), nitrogen (fertilisers), chlorine (purification), iodine (antiseptic).")]),
("h2", "Exploratory projects"),
("ul", ["Find the states of Dhokra, Bidriware, Pembarthi and Kamrupi metal art and make a collage.", "Mark the states where iron, gold and aluminium are found on a map of India.", "List metals and non-metals used in a smartphone.", "Debate: should metals for comfort and luxury be used more or less?"]),
],
takeaways=["Metals are lustrous, hard, **malleable, ductile, sonorous** and good conductors of **heat and electricity**.", "Non-metals are generally dull, brittle or soft and poor conductors.", "Iron **rusts** only in the presence of **air and water**; corrosion is damage of metal surfaces; prevent with painting, oiling, greasing or galvanisation.", "**Metal oxides are basic; non-metal oxides are acidic.**", "Sodium is stored in kerosene, phosphorus under water.", "**Elements** are the basic substances (118 known) divided into metals and non-metals; plastic, wood, glass are not elements.", "Non-metals like oxygen, carbon, nitrogen, chlorine and iodine are essential for life."])
